+# Feature Specification: JSON Pointer resolution
+
+**Standard**: RFC 6901 (JSON Pointer), sections 3 (syntax), 4 (evaluation), 5 (JSON string representation).
+Out of scope: section 6 (URI fragment representation).
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing
+### Story 1 - Read a value by pointer (P1)
+A service calls `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` and gets the referenced value.
+- `""` returns the whole document; `"/foo/0"` follows object keys and array indexes (RFC 6901 section 5 examples).
+- `~1` decodes to `/` and `~0` to `~` (decoded in that order); `""` key via `"/"`.
+- A value of `null` resolves to `null`.
+
+### Story 2 - Reject bad pointers and unresolvable references (P1)
+Throws `JsonPointerError` when:
+- pointer is non-empty and does not start with `/`, or contains `~` not followed by `0` or `1` (section 3);
+- a token is not found (missing object key, array index >= length), or `-` is used (nonexistent element, section 4);
+- array token is not `0` or digits without leading zeros (section 4);
+- traversal reaches a primitive or `null`.
+
+## Requirements
+- FR-1 Valid pointers evaluate per section 4; the empty pointer returns the document.
+- FR-2 Invalid syntax throws `JsonPointerError`; unresolvable throws `JsonPointerError`.
+- FR-3 Only own properties of objects resolve (`__proto__`, `constructor`, `prototype` are not reachable unless own keys); no mutation of inputs, and the returned value is the referenced one (not copied).
+- FR-4 Non-string `pointer` throws `JsonPointerError`.
+
+## Assumptions
+- Object keys are matched exactly, case-sensitive, duplicates not applicable to parsed JS values.
+- Public API addition only (minor, no breaking change). README updated.
+
+## Success Criteria
+- All RFC 6901 section 5 examples pass; every error class above is covered by a test.
